#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276} :activity/id #uuid "9b33318d-1bab-5993-91f6-d6c8c1d3d0a0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148743 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e276ddb-1c14-51fd-8261-8c02eb3294f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3803 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1ae4dc56-971c-585e-ac6e-a1d1927975cc" :step/math-academy-id 21910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147341 :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421902}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "27bcbb90-63f9-5635-829b-a801f3809b3e" :step/math-academy-id 21917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147342 :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393126}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "0ba22f44-3353-5e3e-a64c-1e09fd59d6d9" :step/math-academy-id 21951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147343 :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "e4a1265b-c056-54ef-b3c5-eaaf48e49ec3" :step/math-academy-id 21954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148744 :tutorial/content "In general, given two planes \n\n$$\n{\\Pi_{{1}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{1}x+b_{1}y + c_{1}z+ d_1=0 \\qquad  \\text{and} \\qquad   {\\Pi_{{2}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{2}x+b_{2}y + c_{2}z+ d_2=0,\n$$\n\n we have the following cases, regarding the positions of their normal vectors  $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2 \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- *Case 1*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\nparallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ then ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$   and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ intersect on a straight line.\n\n- *Case 2*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\parallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ but the corresponding equations of the planes are not scalar multiples of each other, then the planes are distinct and are parallel to each other.\n\n- *Case 3*:  If the corresponding coefficients of ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ are all proportional, then the planes are coincident." :tutorial/id #uuid "50ffbdb5-c9d0-5815-9b29-b505cde7f0fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3804 :tutorial/title "Intersection of Two Planes in 3D"} :step/id #uuid "cba5c95d-7e83-5872-ae95-b6832ff486a2" :step/math-academy-id 21955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148745 :tutorial/content "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7303bab-ef2c-5a9b-9caa-dc6fcc7929d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3817 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "8aec20bb-3034-54e2-b58d-43a8fc04ec0d" :step/math-academy-id 22145}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147718}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906} :activity/id #uuid "39b191e6-3a28-55d0-8f36-ad23b66bbcd4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a139c706-0e77-54a4-accb-bfe443670e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453922} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0a00e-bd44-522f-87a0-1661c0717d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453922} :answer-field/id #uuid "227c754c-4dc6-54d5-bd20-dc203c3bd56d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86a8ed31-2bd6-5da1-bce7-520c67f0b586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453924} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef7da89-3321-5d51-9ea4-d7214de7dcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453925} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5956ce-b17c-56a5-9c55-441171fc7b01" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c079efb-b705-50e6-8fa4-f8d29cbaf064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453926} {:answer/id #uuid "313e94f5-659f-51cb-b0fd-10e36f25ce06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453926} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf7ace9d-04e4-53a1-8f3e-3670bba92643" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" :question/math-academy-id "q-71465" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- **II.** The planes intersect on a line.\n\n- **III.** The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "0311a340-7168-5cea-821e-a5c70f05f78d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbb0f75-5837-5fef-9156-1bb0626a6799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260264} {:answer/id #uuid "358aeb92-2af7-5b63-a3fc-98a16739711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260355} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae1bb4f-6418-5cbe-8748-f187b44dbec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} {:answer/id #uuid "83b18a92-b695-5c83-b721-b2dee329f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261019} {:answer/id #uuid "db99d772-027a-5c17-b647-51f20577ed39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5261fb-a30e-5c21-b272-d448a6432184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad00dc96-c374-5320-be1d-ca73d7e96f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-129238" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "4b62f78b-d432-5341-a2aa-4388a4c716e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2afd41-011d-50b7-bdd6-b2fe0e79319a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451519} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a8a6a1-4d09-5b9a-87bf-b8affe56cc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451519} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ff5205-ba68-5bf5-9b0a-caec3fa1d55c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4573706-4ddd-5c59-ae75-f76209f442b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451521} {:answer/id #uuid "8a64a65e-cbd5-5aa2-9fc1-b39766e3b1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451521} :answer-field/id #uuid "a02ea405-f5ad-5f66-b8b5-c7c965e15944" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86e1367f-c77e-5db8-87a9-df2724a72ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451523} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf169b7-e727-58e0-a834-1eca473c044e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451524} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ecb6f3e-52a7-56a2-9a87-95a8d2a508e8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "894f2787-9676-52de-a718-3b82f8fd7a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71463" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n\n- **II.** The planes are parallel.\n\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "8993a320-0fa1-5bea-8343-47ddcfb4405b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a5b51a0-3005-5ddd-b5ab-b8d3b530fe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421890} {:answer/id #uuid "77d58298-86f5-5da7-a6f5-5aadeb1af6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-1,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421891} {:answer/id #uuid "782abb1a-40ca-50f3-9eb2-460b14bea776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421892} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4a733d-fbda-5f24-ba39-11bd9f15b6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421893} {:answer/id #uuid "d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9b9e23-e285-51ba-8f2e-0f93320571fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3582cc5d-9e46-5e0a-8d0c-b0e74f8f5924" :question/math-academy-id "q-71349" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-1=0$ and $x+z+1=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+y-1=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+1=0\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩+t⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+(-1)=0,$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "b2250ab1-dcbf-5807-a3a8-ee6a40866bf6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc5a66d-b1b1-585a-b0bc-21d19ca71687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421889} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3c1355-587b-53a6-a6d7-2db08ae599af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421894} {:answer/id #uuid "d155552d-65e9-593d-bf4d-41010a2e061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421895} {:answer/id #uuid "e23fcb96-7207-5675-97d0-1ce03137e6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b80375-370c-5de5-b724-efe10f25c766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=10,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea7962c-6925-55c2-86b0-52523c308e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "caefbcdd-6450-5b4b-b965-772f5b87b02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129208" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x+3y-3=0$ and $3x+z+19=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+3y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+19=0\\,⟹\\,z=-19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-19⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3y-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+z+19=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-2j-9k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-19⟩+t⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=1-(-19)=20,$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "c6d8b30b-6627-55c2-a300-1bd06da939e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904}}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421888}]}
